steps {
  type: CORTEX_STEP_TYPE_USER_INPUT
  status: CORTEX_STEP_STATUS_DONE
  metadata {
    created_at {
      seconds: 1784994391
      nanos: 955275754
    }
    source: CORTEX_STEP_SOURCE_USER_EXPLICIT
  }
  user_input {
    user_response: "**Build a brand-new, universal, cross-platform desktop application for AI-powered file sorting.**\n\nThis is a new application designed from the ground up to run natively on **macOS, Windows, and Linux** from a single codebase (not a port of an existing macOS app). Use the attached HTML web app only as a **reference for the intended UI and functionality**. Build it using **Tauri 2** with a **Rust backend** and reuse/adapt the HTML/CSS/JS as the frontend. Do **not**use Electron or ship a browser-wrapped web page. Ensure the file monitoring, file operations, and permissions layers are abstracted so they work correctly on all three operating systems.\n\nThe app integrates with a **local Ollama instance** for all AI-driven sorting logic.\n\n1. AI Integration\n\n- Use the local Ollama HTTP API (via the Rust backend, e.g. `reqwest`) for all sorting analysis.\n- **Ollama connection details (host, port, and optional API key for authenticated/proxied setups) are configured only in the Settings tab.**\n- A **persistent top bar**, visible across all tabs, provides quick controls that mirror saved settings:\n  - A dropdown to select the active Ollama model (populated by querying Ollama\'s available models).\n  - A toggle between **Auto-Sort** and **Review** mode.\n- **File content analysis:** For text-based files, extract and send the text to Ollama. For non-text files (images, PDFs, video, binaries), sort by filename, extension, and metadata; optionally use an Ollama vision model for images if one is selected.\n\n2. Tabbed Interface\n\n- **Watch Tab:** Live statistics (files sorted today, total sorted, success/failure counts, current queue depth) and real-time per-watch-folder status. Read-only; folders are created in the Rules tab.\n- **Shelf Tab:** Each configured target folder and all files successfully sorted into their AI-created subfolders.\n- **Rules Tab:** Create, edit, delete sorting rules. Each rule defines a **Source Folder** and a **Target Folder**; the AI determines and creates the appropriate subfolder inside the Target Folder. Multiple rules run independently and simultaneously.\n- **Activity Tab:** Chronological log of every sort (original path, destination subfolder, timestamp) with **Undo** per entry and bulk undo.\n- **Settings Tab:** Ollama connection parameters (host, port, optional API key, default model) and Watch Behavior (OS-native file-system events by default, with configurable polling-interval fallback).\n\n3. File Operation Rules\n\n- **Move** files (do not copy).\n- **Duplicate filenames:** append an incrementing number (`report.pdf` \342\206\222 `report (1).pdf`).\n- **Files in use / locked:** skip and log a warning to the UI; retry next cycle.\n- **Large files:** support up to **4 GB**.\n- **Failed sorts:** log to the UI with the reason; never lose or partially move a file.\n- **Undo:** maintain an undo journal so any sort can be reversed.\n\n4. Permissions &amp; Persistence\n\n- On first run, and whenever a new folder/drive is referenced, trigger the appropriate OS-native permission prompt for folder/drive access. Handle macOS (security-scoped access), Windows, and Linux permission models correctly.\n- Persist **user settings and rules** in a per-OS config file (TOML/JSON) in the standard app-config directory (`~/Library/Application Support/` on macOS, `%APPDATA%` on Windows, `~/.config/` on Linux \342\200\224 resolved via Tauri\'s path APIs).\n- Persist **activity history and the undo journal** in a local **SQLite** database.\n- Monitoring resumes automatically for all rules when the app restarts.\n\n5. Implementation Requirements\n\n- All file operations and AI processing must be real \342\200\224 no simulated, placeholder, or mock logic.\n- Each watch folder operates independently and must not interfere with others.\n- Use OS-native file-system events for efficient monitoring (FSEvents / ReadDirectoryChangesW / inotify via the Rust `notify` crate), with polling as a configurable fallback.\n- Robust cross-platform error handling for all file operations and Ollama API calls, surfaced clearly in the UI.\n- UI follows native desktop conventions on each platform, supports light/dark mode, and stays responsive during large-file operations and AI calls (run these off the main thread)."
    items {
      text: "**Build a brand-new, universal, cross-platform desktop application for AI-powered file sorting.**\n\nThis is a new application designed from the ground up to run natively on **macOS, Windows, and Linux** from a single codebase (not a port of an existing macOS app). Use the attached HTML web app only as a **reference for the intended UI and functionality**. Build it using **Tauri 2** with a **Rust backend** and reuse/adapt the HTML/CSS/JS as the frontend. Do **not**use Electron or ship a browser-wrapped web page. Ensure the file monitoring, file operations, and permissions layers are abstracted so they work correctly on all three operating systems.\n\nThe app integrates with a **local Ollama instance** for all AI-driven sorting logic.\n\n1. AI Integration\n\n- Use the local Ollama HTTP API (via the Rust backend, e.g. `reqwest`) for all sorting analysis.\n- **Ollama connection details (host, port, and optional API key for authenticated/proxied setups) are configured only in the Settings tab.**\n- A **persistent top bar**, visible across all tabs, provides quick controls that mirror saved settings:\n  - A dropdown to select the active Ollama model (populated by querying Ollama\'s available models).\n  - A toggle between **Auto-Sort** and **Review** mode.\n- **File content analysis:** For text-based files, extract and send the text to Ollama. For non-text files (images, PDFs, video, binaries), sort by filename, extension, and metadata; optionally use an Ollama vision model for images if one is selected.\n\n2. Tabbed Interface\n\n- **Watch Tab:** Live statistics (files sorted today, total sorted, success/failure counts, current queue depth) and real-time per-watch-folder status. Read-only; folders are created in the Rules tab.\n- **Shelf Tab:** Each configured target folder and all files successfully sorted into their AI-created subfolders.\n- **Rules Tab:** Create, edit, delete sorting rules. Each rule defines a **Source Folder** and a **Target Folder**; the AI determines and creates the appropriate subfolder inside the Target Folder. Multiple rules run independently and simultaneously.\n- **Activity Tab:** Chronological log of every sort (original path, destination subfolder, timestamp) with **Undo** per entry and bulk undo.\n- **Settings Tab:** Ollama connection parameters (host, port, optional API key, default model) and Watch Behavior (OS-native file-system events by default, with configurable polling-interval fallback).\n\n3. File Operation Rules\n\n- **Move** files (do not copy).\n- **Duplicate filenames:** append an incrementing number (`report.pdf` \342\206\222 `report (1).pdf`).\n- **Files in use / locked:** skip and log a warning to the UI; retry next cycle.\n- **Large files:** support up to **4 GB**.\n- **Failed sorts:** log to the UI with the reason; never lose or partially move a file.\n- **Undo:** maintain an undo journal so any sort can be reversed.\n\n4. Permissions &amp; Persistence\n\n- On first run, and whenever a new folder/drive is referenced, trigger the appropriate OS-native permission prompt for folder/drive access. Handle macOS (security-scoped access), Windows, and Linux permission models correctly.\n- Persist **user settings and rules** in a per-OS config file (TOML/JSON) in the standard app-config directory (`~/Library/Application Support/` on macOS, `%APPDATA%` on Windows, `~/.config/` on Linux \342\200\224 resolved via Tauri\'s path APIs).\n- Persist **activity history and the undo journal** in a local **SQLite** database.\n- Monitoring resumes automatically for all rules when the app restarts.\n\n5. Implementation Requirements\n\n- All file operations and AI processing must be real \342\200\224 no simulated, placeholder, or mock logic.\n- Each watch folder operates independently and must not interfere with others.\n- Use OS-native file-system events for efficient monitoring (FSEvents / ReadDirectoryChangesW / inotify via the Rust `notify` crate), with polling as a configurable fallback.\n- Robust cross-platform error handling for all file operations and Ollama API calls, surfaced clearly in the UI.\n- UI follows native desktop conventions on each platform, supports light/dark mode, and stays responsive during large-file operations and AI calls (run these off the main thread)."
    }
    media {
      mime_type: "text/html"
      blobref {
        blob_id: "/blobstore/prod/makersuite/spanner_managed/global::000054e2ea70026d:0000015f:2:000054e2ea70026d:0000000000000001::4b37630e30704845:00000211878018bc:0006577164a1544f"
      }
      uri: "QwenAutoSort.html"
    }
  }
}
