  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Analyzing the Visual Cutoff**\n\nI\'m revisiting the user\'s description. The key issue appears to be a visual cutoff. I\'m focusing on why the box might be truncated. I am looking at the `App.tsx` file for possible causes. I suspect the height might be the issue, or perhaps some CSS settings. I will look for clues in the rendering logic.\n\n\n**Investigating the Vertical Layout**\n\nI\'ve zeroed in on the vertical centering issue. The `min-h-[620px]` and `h-[90vh]` combined with `items-center justify-center` might be the problem. The viewport\'s height, especially in smaller viewports like an iframe, is critical. A negative Y offset can cause the top of the box to be out of view. I need to test scenarios where the box\'s minimum height is close to or taller than the viewport. I\'ll check `html`, `body`, and `#root` for default CSS height constraints.\n\n\n**Refining the Height Constraints**\n\nI\'ve made some significant adjustments. I\'ve set `html`, `body`, and `#root` to `height: 100%`, `margin: 0`, and `padding: 0` and `overflow: hidden` in `src/index.css`. In `App.tsx`, I changed the outer div to `h-screen w-screen` and the inner to `h-full max-h-[calc(100vh-2.5rem)]`. The goal is now a viewport-filling container that dynamically adjusts the box\'s height, avoiding the visual cutoff and ensuring margins. This should dynamically scale the box within the viewport.\n\n\n**Confirming the Vertical Solution**\n\nI\'m verifying the height constraints now. Since `h-full max-h-[calc(100vh-2.5rem)]` is capped by the viewport and padding, it will never exceed the screen, preventing any cutoff. I also confirmed that each tab view component has a `max-h` calculation to manage scrolling. I\'m focusing on those view components\' `flex-1 overflow-y-auto h-full` and how the main view and tab views scroll dynamically. I confirmed the view components all correctly scroll within their view.\n\n\n**Optimizing the Scroll Behavior**\n\nI\'ve streamlined the view container\'s behavior. Replacing `max-h-[calc(100vh-120px)]` with `h-full` in `SettingsView` and other components makes them scroll cleanly within the panel without the old miscalculations. They now fill and scroll correctly, which eliminates the overflow issue. I\'ve successfully cleaned up the scroll behavior.\n\n\\n\\n\n\n**Removing the Gemini Integration**\n\nI\'m removing the Gemini AI integration per the user\'s instructions. In `server.ts`, I\'ve removed the `@google/genai` import, the `useGeminiFallback` setting from the database and defaults, the `genAIClient` and `getGeminiClient()` functions, and the `hasGeminiKey` flag from the `/api/state` endpoint. I\'ve also removed the `/api/gemini/classify` route entirely. All related code has been removed.\n\n\n**Refactoring the Type Definitions**\n\nI\'ve updated `src/types.ts`. I\'ve removed \'gemini\' from the `SortingEngine` type and deleted `useGeminiFallback` and `hasGeminiKey` from the `Settings` interface. I\'ve removed any related `hasGeminiKey` references from other interfaces, if present. I\'m streamlining the codebase to reflect the AI removal.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 7
      nanos: 885353688
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
